# Debugging as a Science
@mastery L3
@prereq why-meta-skills-are-the-moat

:::: grounding
includes: Debugging as a repeatable, falsifiable procedure — hypothesis-driven investigation and bisection over a search space.
know: Debugging is applied science — form a falsifiable hypothesis about the cause, design the smallest experiment that could disprove it, and only then change code. [zeller-why-programs-fail]
know: Bisecting over the space of possible causes (commits, inputs, code paths, time) turns an O(n) hunt into an O(log n) hunt — the identical complexity win as any binary search over a sorted range. [git-bisect-docs]
know: A bug is not "fixed" until it has been reliably reproduced and the fix has been checked against that reproduction — an unverified fix is a guess wearing a diff. [zeller-why-programs-fail]
misconception: Skilled engineers debug faster because they read code faster. | Skilled engineers debug faster because they guess less. They spend their time forming and testing hypotheses instead of staring at code hoping the bug reveals itself by inspection.
misconception: Print statements and breakpoints are beginner tools that senior engineers grow out of. | Print statements, breakpoints, and structured logging are exactly how senior engineers gather evidence. The skill was never the tool — it's using the tool to test one specific hypothesis instead of scattering instrumentation everywhere and hoping something looks wrong.
source: zeller-why-programs-fail | Andreas Zeller, "Why Programs Fail: A Guide to Systematic Debugging" | https://www.whyprogramsfail.com/
source: git-bisect-docs | Git Documentation, "git-bisect" | https://git-scm.com/docs/git-bisect
::::

Picture the bug that will actually teach you this: something in DeskFlow's terminal agent pipeline fails maybe one run in fifteen, and every time you glance at the code it "looks fine." This is the exact situation where debugging skill and debugging *luck* diverge. Luck stares at the code and eventually spots it. Skill runs a procedure that finds it regardless of how well-hidden it is — and finds it in bounded time, not open-ended time.

The procedure is the scientific method with the nouns swapped: **observe → hypothesize → predict → experiment → revise.** You observe the actual failure (not your memory of it, not a description of it — the real stack trace, the real malformed output). You form one specific, falsifiable hypothesis: "the PTY write buffer overflows when the agent's JSON payload exceeds N bytes," not "something's wrong with the terminal." A good hypothesis makes a prediction you didn't already know: *if this is true, then forcing a payload past N bytes should reproduce it every time.* Then you run exactly that experiment. If it reproduces, you've converted a 1-in-15 mystery into a 100% reproducible one — which is the actual milestone, more important than the fix itself. If it doesn't reproduce, the hypothesis is dead; you don't patch around it, you form the next one. This loop is why debugging feels slow when you're bad at it (random edits, hoping) and fast once you're good at it (each step eliminates a real chunk of the hypothesis space).

Bisection is the same idea applied to a search space instead of a single hypothesis. When you don't know *which* commit introduced a regression, don't read commits in order — that's a linear scan. Test the midpoint commit; its result (good/bad) eliminates half the remaining history in one shot, exactly like binary search over a sorted array. `git bisect` automates this, but the *idea* generalizes past commits: bisecting over inputs (does it fail on this half of the dataset or that half?), over code paths (comment out this half of the pipeline — still fails?), and over time (did it work yesterday? this morning? ten minutes ago?) are all the same move.

::: figure Binary search over commit history — each test halves the remaining suspects
<svg viewBox="0 0 640 260" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
  <style>
    .lbl { font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px; fill: #222; font-weight: 600; }
    .cap { font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 12px; fill: #666; }
  </style>
  <text x="10" y="20" class="lbl">Round 1 — 64 suspect commits</text>
  <rect x="10" y="30" width="600" height="20" fill="#e6e6e6" stroke="#999"/>
  <rect x="10" y="30" width="20" height="20" fill="#4caf50"/>
  <rect x="590" y="30" width="20" height="20" fill="#e53935"/>
  <circle cx="310" cy="40" r="6" fill="#222"/>
  <text x="300" y="65" class="cap">test commit #32 → bad</text>

  <text x="10" y="100" class="lbl">Round 2 — 32 suspect commits</text>
  <rect x="10" y="110" width="300" height="20" fill="#e6e6e6" stroke="#999"/>
  <rect x="10" y="110" width="20" height="20" fill="#4caf50"/>
  <rect x="290" y="110" width="20" height="20" fill="#e53935"/>
  <circle cx="160" cy="120" r="6" fill="#222"/>
  <text x="150" y="145" class="cap">test commit #16 → good</text>

  <text x="10" y="180" class="lbl">Round 3 — 16 suspect commits</text>
  <rect x="160" y="190" width="150" height="20" fill="#e6e6e6" stroke="#999"/>
  <rect x="160" y="190" width="20" height="20" fill="#4caf50"/>
  <rect x="290" y="190" width="20" height="20" fill="#e53935"/>
  <circle cx="235" cy="200" r="6" fill="#222"/>
  <text x="225" y="225" class="cap">test commit #24 → bad → 8 commits remain</text>

  <text x="10" y="250" class="cap">⌈log₂64⌉ = 6 tests total finds the exact commit — vs. up to 64 if you read serially.</text>
</svg>
:::

This is also why reproduction is non-negotiable before you touch a fix. A fix applied to a bug you can't reliably reproduce is unfalsifiable — you have no way to distinguish "I fixed it" from "it happened not to occur in my next three test runs," and intermittent bugs occur intermittently *precisely* in the cases people declare victory too early. The uncomfortable corollary: sometimes the highest-value hour you spend on a nasty bug is entirely invested in reproduction, with zero lines of the actual fix written. That hour is not wasted time — it's the only thing that makes the next hour meaningful.

::: callout warning
Watch for the "shotgun fix" anti-pattern: changing five things at once because one of them is probably related. Even if it makes the symptom disappear, you now don't know which change did it, which means you've learned nothing and the other four changes are unverified risk you just merged into the codebase.
:::

**Build-to-learn:** Take the last genuinely confusing bug you fixed in DeskFlow or NeuroQuant — the one where you're honestly not 100% sure your fix was the real cause. Write down, after the fact, the hypothesis you'd have needed and the single experiment that would have confirmed it in one shot. That reconstruction is the skill; do it once deliberately and it starts happening automatically during the *next* bug.

::: quiz numeric L2
A regression could be in any of 500 commits. Using bisection, what's the maximum number of tests needed (worst case) to find the exact commit?
answer: 9
explain: ⌈log₂500⌉ = 9. Each test halves the remaining range regardless of which half turns out to be bad — that's what makes the worst case bounded and small.
:::

::: quiz mcq L2
An API call returns wrong data roughly 1 in 20 requests. What's the FIRST move of a scientific debugger?
- [ ] Wrap the call in a try/catch and swallow the error
- [ ] Read the whole call stack top to bottom looking for the bug
- [x] Find a reliable way to reproduce the failure, even if it takes real engineering effort
- [ ] Rewrite the function since it's clearly unreliable
explain: Without reproduction, no hypothesis is testable — every "fix" is unverifiable. A 1-in-20 rate usually signals a race condition or an environment-dependent input; reproducing it IS the investigation.
:::

::: quiz open L4
Describe a bug where bisecting over inputs (not commits) would be the right move, and explain what makes a "good" midpoint choice in that space.
rubric: A concrete scenario (e.g., NeuroQuant's signal fusion breaks on some historical windows but not others). A midpoint that actually splits the remaining hypothesis space roughly in half — not just "the middle date" but a split informed by a suspected variable (data density, a specific instrument's gaps, a volatility regime change).
:::

::: layer L5 deeper
**Delta debugging (ddmin).** Manual bisection halves a *linear* range. Delta debugging generalizes the idea to any structured input: given a failing input (a long JSON payload, a long log, a big test case), automatically remove chunks and re-test, converging on the *minimal* failing input — often just the handful of bytes actually responsible. This is how compiler and browser engineers reduce a 50,000-line crash report to the three lines that matter, and it is directly applicable to shrinking a failing DeskFlow agent transcript down to the exact malformed fragment. Andreas Zeller's original ddmin algorithm is the canonical reference — worth implementing once from scratch; it's a page of code and it will change how you think about every future crash report.
:::

