# Reading Source Code & Papers
@mastery L3
@prereq why-meta-skills-are-the-moat

:::: grounding
includes: Reading disciplines for source code and academic papers — non-linear reading order and effort allocation.
know: Read non-linearly. For a paper: title → abstract → headings → conclusion → skim references first (Keshav's "three-pass" method), not front-to-back. For code: find the entry point and trace one real execution path before reading anything else. [keshav-how-to-read]
know: Passing only the first pass of a paper tells you its category and claim, not whether the claim holds — you have not "read" a paper until at least the second pass, where you evaluate the evidence. [keshav-how-to-read]
know: Tracing one representative execution through unfamiliar code — with a debugger or a few well-placed log lines — teaches more in ten minutes than an hour of static top-to-bottom reading, because it shows you which of the many code paths actually matter. [seibel-coders-at-work]
misconception: Reading source code means reading every line, in file order. | Reading source code means finding the entry point, tracing one representative call through it, and zooming in only on the parts that surprised you. Most of any real codebase is irrelevant to the question you actually came with.
misconception: Skimming the abstract and intro counts as "having read" a paper. | That's Keshav's first pass only — five to ten minutes that tells you the category and headline claim. You cannot defend or attack the method, spot a weak baseline, or cite it accurately from a first pass alone.
source: keshav-how-to-read | S. Keshav, "How to Read a Paper," ACM SIGCOMM Computer Communication Review | https://doi.org/10.1145/1273445.1273458
source: seibel-coders-at-work | Peter Seibel, "Coders at Work" | https://www.codersatwork.com/
::::

You already do a version of this every time you open someone else's repo to steal an idea for DeskFlow — the question is whether you're doing it as a procedure or as a vibe. The procedure is what turns "I skimmed it" into "I could reimplement it," and the gap between those two is most of this node.

Start with papers, because the three-pass structure is the cleanest version of the idea. **Pass one** (five to ten minutes) is title, abstract, section headings, and the conclusion — read in that order, skipping the body entirely — plus a skim of the reference list for names you recognize. The goal is a single sentence: what category of problem is this, and what's the headline claim. If you can't produce that sentence, you don't yet know whether this paper is even relevant to what you're working on, and reading further is premature. **Pass two** (about an hour) reads the body with real attention but *ignores proofs and derivations* — you're not verifying yet, you're understanding the shape of the argument, and critically, you study every figure and table closely, because that's usually where the actual result lives, with the prose around it doing PR for it. You note which references you haven't read; that's how a literature map gets built incrementally instead of all at once. **Pass three** (hours, and only for papers that matter enough) is a virtual reimplementation: same assumptions the authors made, but you re-derive their results yourself, which is the only way hidden assumptions, weak citations, and untested edge cases surface. Most engineers do a bad, hurried version of pass one and call it "read the paper." Most of the value is in choosing which papers earn a pass three — that choice is itself the taste skill from later in this part.

::: figure Three passes, not one linear read — most people stop at a bad imitation of Pass 1
<svg viewBox="0 0 640 420" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
  <style>.t{font-family:sans-serif;} .h{font-size:15px;font-weight:bold;fill:#222;} .b{font-size:12px;fill:#444;}</style>
  <rect x="20" y="20" width="600" height="380" rx="12" fill="#fdf6ec" stroke="#e0a94a" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="40" y="50" class="t h">Pass 1 — Skim (5–10 min)</text>
  <text x="40" y="72" class="t b">Title, abstract, section headings, conclusion. Skim references for familiar names.</text>
  <text x="40" y="90" class="t b">Goal: state the category, claim, and contribution in one sentence.</text>

  <rect x="60" y="110" width="560" height="260" rx="12" fill="#eef6ee" stroke="#5a9a5a" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="80" y="140" class="t h">Pass 2 — Read with care (~1 hr)</text>
  <text x="80" y="162" class="t b">Read the body, ignore proofs/derivations. Study every figure, diagram, and table.</text>
  <text x="80" y="180" class="t b">Note unread references — this is how a literature map gets built.</text>
  <text x="80" y="198" class="t b">Goal: summarize the main thrust to a colleague, with supporting evidence.</text>

  <rect x="100" y="220" width="480" height="130" rx="12" fill="#eaf0fb" stroke="#4c7bd6" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="120" y="250" class="t h">Pass 3 — Re-derive (hours)</text>
  <text x="120" y="272" class="t b">Virtually reimplement it: same assumptions, re-derive the results yourself.</text>
  <text x="120" y="290" class="t b">Identify hidden assumptions, weak citations, untested edge cases.</text>
  <text x="120" y="308" class="t b">Goal: reimplement from memory and know exactly where it's fragile.</text>

  <text x="40" y="400" class="t b">Reserve Pass 3 for the small number of papers that actually matter to what you're building.</text>
</svg>
:::

Source code inverts the order but keeps the spirit: start from the *outside* — the public interface, the entry point, a call graph one level deep — before you drop into implementation. Ask "what does this promise the caller?" before "how does it deliver on that promise?" Then trace exactly one real execution path end to end, using a debugger or deliberate logging, for the specific case you care about. This is a bisection move in disguise: instead of reading every branch, you're eliminating the branches your actual scenario never touches. Only once you understand that one path do you zoom into the parts that surprised you — the places where the code did something you didn't predict, because those are precisely the places your mental model was wrong, and reading is only valuable where it corrects a wrong model.

::: callout tip
When you evaluate an open-source dependency you're about to pull into DeskFlow or FM-AI, do one abbreviated pass-two pass on its core module before trusting it: skim the tests (they show you the *intended* behavior faster than the implementation does), then trace one real call. Fifteen minutes here has saved people from load-bearing bugs in libraries with 40k GitHub stars.
:::

**Build-to-learn:** Pick one paper genuinely relevant to NeuroQuant or DeskFlow's RAG pipeline — something you've been citing from memory but never actually re-derived. Run the full three-pass method on it, and for pass three, actually reimplement its core mechanism (even a toy version) in code. This is the exact seed North Star names for this part, and it's the single highest-leverage afternoon you can spend on this skill.

::: quiz mcq L2
You need to decide whether a paper is worth a full re-derivation (pass three). Which pass tells you that?
- [ ] Pass one — the abstract and conclusion alone
- [x] Pass two — after seeing the actual evidence and figures
- [ ] You should always do pass three for anything cited more than once
- [ ] Reading the appendix first
explain: Pass one only tells you what's being claimed, not whether the evidence supports it or whether it's load-bearing for your work. That judgment call requires having actually seen the figures and the argument's structure.
:::

::: quiz open L3
Describe your procedure for reading an unfamiliar module in someone else's codebase before you trust it enough to depend on it in DeskFlow.
rubric: Interface-first (what's the promise, not the mechanism). Tracing one concrete call path rather than reading top-to-bottom. Checking tests as a faster proxy for intended behavior. Zooming into surprises, not everything uniformly.
:::

