# Knowing What NOT to Build
@mastery L3
@prereq taste-judgment

:::: grounding
includes: Scoping judgment — evaluating whether a feature's forever-cost is worth its value, and writing explicit non-goals.
know: Every feature carries a cost forever after it ships — maintenance, tests, docs, support, and the cognitive load of a bigger surface area — regardless of how cheap it was to write in the first place. [ousterhout-philosophy-of-software-design]
know: An explicit "non-goal" in a spec — "we are deliberately not solving X here" — is what actually stops scope creep, and is often more valuable to a reviewer than any stated goal in the same document. [google-design-doc-practices]
misconception: Turning down a feature request is a failure of ambition, or evidence you can't build it. | Saying no to a low-value, high-effort feature is a design decision, applying the same judgment you'd apply to an architecture choice — just aimed at scope instead of code.
misconception: More features always mean more value delivered to the user. | Past a point, more features mean more surface area to secure, document, and maintain, and more decisions a user has to make before doing the one thing they actually came to do. Net value can go negative well before a product feels "done."
source: ousterhout-philosophy-of-software-design | John Ousterhout, "A Philosophy of Software Design" | https://web.stanford.edu/~ouster/cgi-bin/book.php
source: google-design-doc-practices | Malte Ubl, "Design Docs at Google" | https://www.industrialempathy.com/posts/design-docs-at-google/
::::

Your own North Star document already names the trap by its actual name: yak-shaving on foundational tooling instead of shipping the flagship product. But the deeper skill isn't "avoid distraction" — it's a specific, evaluable judgment call you make dozens of times a week, usually without noticing you're making it: *for this candidate feature, is the value it returns worth the cost it locks in forever?* Not the cost to build it once — the cost to carry it. Every feature you ship becomes a promise: it will keep working, keep being documented, keep being compatible with whatever you build next, and keep being one more thing a new contributor (or future-you, six months from now) has to understand before they can safely change anything nearby.

This reframes "no" from a failure into an active design decision with the same shape as picking an architecture. When you reject a data structure because it doesn't fit the access pattern, nobody calls that a failure of ambition — it's engineering judgment. Rejecting a feature request for the identical reason (it doesn't fit the cost/value the product can afford right now) deserves the same respect, and treating it that way is most of what separates a scoped, shippable roadmap from an ever-growing backlog that never converges.

::: chart
{
  "$schema": "https://vega.github.io/schema/vega-lite/v5.json",
  "title": "Candidate DeskFlow features — effort vs. impact",
  "description": "The shaded corner (high effort, low impact) is what NOT to build right now",
  "data": {"values": [
    {"name": "Custom in-house auth system", "effort": 9, "impact": 2},
    {"name": "Config-driven plugin marketplace", "effort": 8, "impact": 3},
    {"name": "One-click PTY session sharing", "effort": 4, "impact": 8},
    {"name": "Shader polish pass #4", "effort": 6, "impact": 2},
    {"name": "Basic onboarding checklist", "effort": 2, "impact": 7},
    {"name": "Custom database engine", "effort": 10, "impact": 1},
    {"name": "Keyboard shortcut palette", "effort": 3, "impact": 6}
  ]},
  "layer": [
    {"mark": {"type": "rule", "color": "#999999", "strokeDash": [4, 4]}, "encoding": {"x": {"datum": 5}}},
    {"mark": {"type": "rule", "color": "#999999", "strokeDash": [4, 4]}, "encoding": {"y": {"datum": 5}}},
    {
      "mark": {"type": "circle", "size": 160},
      "encoding": {
        "x": {"field": "effort", "type": "quantitative", "title": "Effort to build", "scale": {"domain": [0, 10]}},
        "y": {"field": "impact", "type": "quantitative", "title": "Impact / value", "scale": {"domain": [0, 10]}},
        "color": {
          "condition": {"test": "datum.effort > 5 && datum.impact < 5", "value": "#d9534f"},
          "value": "#4c8bf5"
        },
        "tooltip": [{"field": "name"}, {"field": "effort"}, {"field": "impact"}]
      }
    },
    {
      "mark": {"type": "text", "dy": -12, "fontSize": 10},
      "encoding": {
        "x": {"field": "effort", "type": "quantitative"},
        "y": {"field": "impact", "type": "quantitative"},
        "text": {"field": "name"}
      }
    }
  ],
  "width": 480,
  "height": 360
}
:::

Notice what the chart can't tell you: it can't tell you the impact or effort numbers themselves — that's an estimate, and estimates are exactly where taste and domain judgment (the previous node) do the real work. What the framework buys you is a forcing function: it makes you state the estimate out loud, in front of yourself, instead of building on vibes and discovering the cost eighteen months later when someone asks why DeskFlow has a plugin marketplace nobody uses and three unresolved security issues in it.

The sharpest tool here is the explicit non-goal, and it belongs in writing, not just in your head. "We are building X" invites a reviewer (or a future you) to ask "did you also handle Y?" — and without a written non-goal, the honest answer is always yes eventually, because nothing stops it. "We are building X. We are explicitly not handling Y in this iteration" gives everyone, including you, permission to stop scope from creeping, and it's the single sentence most roadmaps are missing.

::: callout warning
Watch for the version of this trap that looks like discipline: building "just the infrastructure first" so the real feature will supposedly be easy later. Infrastructure built before the feature that needs it is a guess about future requirements, and guesses about requirements are usually wrong in some detail that forces a rewrite anyway. Build the feature; extract the infrastructure once you've built it twice.
:::

**Build-to-learn:** Take your actual DeskFlow or NeuroQuant backlog and run every open item through the effort/impact framework above, explicitly. For anything that lands in the "don't build" quadrant, write the one-sentence non-goal that kills it — not just in your head, in the backlog itself, so the decision is visible and doesn't get silently re-litigated in three months.

::: quiz mcq L3
A teammate proposes a plugin marketplace for DeskFlow. It's technically impressive and would look great in a demo. What's the sharpest question to ask before greenlighting it?
- [ ] How long will it take to build?
- [x] What ongoing cost — security review, docs, compatibility — does this create for every future release, and does the current user base justify that forever-cost?
- [ ] Has anyone else built something like this?
- [ ] Can AI help us build it faster?
explain: Build time is a one-time cost. The real question that determines whether a feature is worth it is the cost it locks in permanently, weighed against actual (not hoped-for) current demand.
:::

::: quiz open L4
Describe a feature you already built into DeskFlow or NeuroQuant that, in hindsight, failed this test — and what non-goal sentence would have prevented it.
rubric: An honest, specific example (not a hypothetical). A concrete forever-cost it created. A precise non-goal sentence that would have stopped it at spec time, not after building it.
:::

::: callout tip
Reinforcement note: this framework only pays off once applied to a real, live decision — not read about. Before moving to the next node, run your actual DeskFlow or NeuroQuant backlog through it right now, while it's fresh. Reading about a decision framework and applying it are different mastery levels on your own scale, and only the second one should move your Progress tracker.
:::

