# Technical Writing & Communication
@mastery L3
@prereq why-meta-skills-are-the-moat

:::: grounding
includes: Technical writing as the mechanism for making engineering judgment reviewable — design docs and conclusion-first structure.
know: A design doc's job is to make your reasoning falsifiable before code is written, when a wrong turn costs an afternoon of discussion instead of a rewrite. [google-design-doc-practices]
know: "Alternatives considered" is the section that proves you actually thought about the problem instead of building the first idea that occurred to you — omitting it is the single most common design-doc failure. [google-design-doc-practices]
know: Good technical writing states the conclusion first, then the supporting reasoning — the opposite order from how you discovered it — because a reader is optimizing to extract the decision, not to relive your journey to it. [google-tech-writing]
misconception: Technical writing is a "soft skill" separate from engineering ability. | Technical writing is the mechanism by which your engineering judgment becomes visible, reviewable, and reusable by anyone who wasn't in your head while you worked it out. Without it, good judgment dies with the pull request that used it.
source: google-design-doc-practices | Malte Ubl, "Design Docs at Google" | https://www.industrialempathy.com/posts/design-docs-at-google/
source: google-tech-writing | Google Developers, "Technical Writing Courses" | https://developers.google.com/tech-writing
::::

You already have a version of this edge — you lead a CS club and you've corrected a university instructor, which means you can already explain things clearly out loud. The gap most self-taught engineers have isn't explaining ability, it's *doc structure discipline*: knowing what a design doc, a PR description, or a build-in-public post is actually *for*, and writing to that purpose instead of narrating your process.

The purpose of a design doc specifically is to move the moment of disagreement earlier, when it's cheap. If you write the code first and explain it after, any objection a reviewer raises means throwing away working code — so objections get suppressed, and bad decisions survive by inertia. If you write the design doc first, the same objection costs a paragraph. This is why "alternatives considered" is the section nearly everyone skips and the one that matters most: it's not padding, it's the proof that you compared options instead of building the first idea that occurred to you, and it's the exact place a sharp reviewer can catch a mistake before it's expensive.

::: html Click each section of a design doc to see what goes there and why
<style>
  .doc-anatomy { font-family: sans-serif; max-width: 600px; }
  .section { border: 1px solid #ccc; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 8px; overflow: hidden; }
  .section-header { background: #f2f2f2; padding: 10px 14px; cursor: pointer; font-weight: 600; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; }
  .section-header:hover { background: #e8e8e8; }
  .section-body { padding: 0 14px; max-height: 0; overflow: hidden; transition: max-height 0.25s ease, padding 0.25s ease; background: #fff; }
  .section.open .section-body { max-height: 300px; padding: 10px 14px; }
  .section-body p { margin: 6px 0; font-size: 13px; color: #333; line-height: 1.4; }
  .tag { font-size: 11px; color: #888; font-weight: 400; }
  .arrow { transition: transform 0.2s ease; font-size: 11px; color: #888; }
  .section.open .arrow { transform: rotate(90deg); }
</style>
<div class="doc-anatomy" id="doc-anatomy">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header"><span>Context &amp; Problem <span class="tag">— why this doc exists</span></span><span class="arrow">▶</span></div>
    <div class="section-body"><p>State the problem so a reader with zero context can follow it. No solution yet — just what's broken, who it hurts, and why now.</p><p>Example: "DeskFlow's agent log parser silently drops malformed JSON lines, hiding real failures from the dashboard."</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header"><span>Goals &amp; Non-Goals <span class="tag">— scope, explicitly</span></span><span class="arrow">▶</span></div>
    <div class="section-body"><p>Goals define success. Non-goals state what you are deliberately NOT solving here — this single sentence is what actually prevents scope creep and unblocks review.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header"><span>Proposed Design <span class="tag">— the "how"</span></span><span class="arrow">▶</span></div>
    <div class="section-body"><p>The mechanism, with a diagram if it's structural. Written so a reviewer can spot the failure mode without running the code.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header"><span>Alternatives Considered <span class="tag">— the part everyone skips</span></span><span class="arrow">▶</span></div>
    <div class="section-body"><p>What else could have worked, and why you rejected it. This is where your judgment becomes visible and reviewable — skipping it is the single most common design-doc mistake.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header"><span>Trade-offs &amp; Risks <span class="tag">— the honesty check</span></span><span class="arrow">▶</span></div>
    <div class="section-body"><p>What does this cost? What could go wrong? A design doc with no listed downside wasn't thought through — it was just written down.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>
<script>
  document.querySelectorAll('.section-header').forEach(function (h) {
    h.addEventListener('click', function () {
      h.parentElement.classList.toggle('open');
      requestAnimationFrame(function () {
        var height = document.getElementById('doc-anatomy').scrollHeight + 40;
        window.parent.postMessage({ type: 'resize', height: height }, '*');
      });
    });
  });
  window.parent.postMessage({ type: 'resize', height: document.getElementById('doc-anatomy').scrollHeight + 40 }, '*');
</script>
:::

The same "conclusion first" principle applies at the sentence level, not just the document level, and it's the part most self-taught writers get backwards because it fights how you actually think through a problem. You discover things in the order you discover them: symptom, then investigation, then cause, then fix. But a reader — a code reviewer, a hiring manager skimming your GitHub, a future collaborator — is not trying to relive your investigation; they're trying to extract the decision as fast as possible and only descend into your reasoning if they doubt it. So the PR description leads with *what changed and why*, not with a chronological account of your debugging session. This is sometimes called "BLUF" (bottom line up front), and it's learnable as a mechanical habit: write the answer sentence first, then go back and write the reasoning underneath it.

::: callout tip
Your build-in-public habit is a technical-writing exercise in disguise, and it's graded by strangers with zero context — which makes it a *harder* version of writing a good PR description, not an easier one. Before you post a commit message or a release note, ask: would this make sense to someone who has never seen DeskFlow before? If not, it's not ready to be public yet, it's a diary entry.
:::

**Build-to-learn:** Pick one real feature you're about to build in DeskFlow or NeuroQuant and write the full design doc above — Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Proposed Design, Alternatives Considered, Trade-offs — *before* you write the code, as if it were going to a strict reviewer. This is the second build-to-learn seed North Star names for this part; do it for real, not as a retroactive writeup of something you already built.

::: quiz mcq L2
Which section of a design doc is most commonly skipped, and is also the one that most reveals whether the author actually exercised judgment?
- [ ] Context & Problem
- [ ] Goals & Non-Goals
- [x] Alternatives Considered
- [ ] Proposed Design
explain: Anyone can describe the problem and describe the solution they picked. Only "alternatives considered" proves you compared options and can defend the choice — which is exactly why weak docs omit it.
:::

::: quiz open L4
Rewrite this PR description to lead with the conclusion instead of the investigation: "I noticed the dashboard was showing stale metrics, so I added some logging, traced it to the cache TTL being set to 24h instead of 5m, and fixed the config value."
rubric: Something like "Fixed stale dashboard metrics caused by a cache TTL misconfiguration (24h instead of 5m)." followed by the investigation as supporting detail, not as the lead sentence.
:::

---

# Taste & Judgment
@mastery L4
@prereq reading-source-code-papers

:::: grounding
includes: Taste as a trainable, explainable prediction about the future consequences of a design choice.
know: Taste in software is measurable indirectly, through consequences — a tangled system needs a meeting to safely change one line; a well-designed one lets one engineer change it alone in an hour. [ousterhout-philosophy-of-software-design]
know: Fred Brooks' "second-system effect" names a specific, recurring taste failure — the tendency to over-engineer the system you build right after you finally understand the problem, because you're eager to use everything you just learned. [brooks-mythical-man-month]
misconception: Taste is subjective, so there's no way to argue about it or teach it. | Taste disagreements usually resolve into an argument about a concrete, checkable consequence — coupling, blast radius, cost of future change. The "feeling" is a compressed prediction about that consequence, and the prediction can be checked against what actually happens later.
misconception: The engineer with the strongest opinions in the room has the best taste. | Taste shows up more reliably in restraint — the engineer who says "this is fine as-is, don't touch it" as often as they push for a rewrite — than in volume or confidence of opinions.
source: ousterhout-philosophy-of-software-design | John Ousterhout, "A Philosophy of Software Design" | https://web.stanford.edu/~ouster/cgi-bin/book.php
source: brooks-mythical-man-month | Fred Brooks, "The Mythical Man-Month" | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Mythical_Man-Month
::::

Your North Star document already names this as a strength you're cultivating, not starting from zero on — which means the job here isn't to build taste from nothing, it's to make it *explainable*, so it survives contact with a code review and doesn't just live as an unexamined gut feeling. That distinction matters because unexamined taste doesn't transfer: you can't teach it to a collaborator, defend it to a reviewer, or even fully trust it yourself under pressure. Explainable taste does all three.

The trick to making taste explainable is realizing it's not actually a feeling — it's a fast, compressed prediction about a concrete future consequence, trained on pattern exposure. When something "feels wrong" about a design, that feeling is standing in for a specific, checkable claim: *this coupling means changing the auth layer will force a change in three unrelated modules,* or *this abstraction hides the one detail callers actually need to know, so they'll all end up working around it.* The move that turns taste into a skill you can improve deliberately is refusing to stop at the feeling — chasing it down to the concrete consequence every time, until doing that becomes fast enough to feel instant again, except now it's checkable.

::: figure Same feature, two designs — the difference shows up as blast radius, not aesthetics
<svg viewBox="0 0 640 320" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
  <style>.t{font-family:sans-serif;font-size:13px;} .h{font-weight:bold;font-size:15px;}</style>
  <text x="30" y="30" class="t h">Same feature, two designs</text>

  <rect x="20" y="50" width="280" height="240" rx="10" fill="#fff5f5" stroke="#e0a0a0"/>
  <text x="40" y="75" class="t h" fill="#b33333">Low taste — tangled</text>
  <circle cx="70" cy="120" r="18" fill="#f2b6b6"/><text x="60" y="125" class="t">UI</text>
  <circle cx="180" cy="120" r="18" fill="#f2b6b6"/><text x="165" y="125" class="t">DB</text>
  <circle cx="260" cy="160" r="18" fill="#f2b6b6"/><text x="243" y="165" class="t">Net</text>
  <circle cx="90" cy="220" r="18" fill="#f2b6b6"/><text x="70" y="225" class="t">Auth</text>
  <circle cx="220" cy="230" r="18" fill="#f2b6b6"/><text x="197" y="235" class="t">Cache</text>
  <line x1="70" y1="120" x2="220" y2="230" stroke="#c66666" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <line x1="180" y1="120" x2="90" y2="220" stroke="#c66666" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <line x1="260" y1="160" x2="70" y2="120" stroke="#c66666" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <line x1="90" y1="220" x2="260" y2="160" stroke="#c66666" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <line x1="180" y1="120" x2="260" y2="160" stroke="#c66666" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <line x1="70" y1="120" x2="90" y2="220" stroke="#c66666" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="40" y="270" class="t">Every box knows about every other box.</text>
  <text x="40" y="286" class="t">Change one — guess what else breaks.</text>

  <rect x="330" y="50" width="280" height="240" rx="10" fill="#f4fbf4" stroke="#a0c8a0"/>
  <text x="350" y="75" class="t h" fill="#3a7a3a">High taste — layered</text>
  <rect x="400" y="100" width="140" height="30" rx="6" fill="#bfe3bf"/><text x="440" y="120" class="t">UI</text>
  <rect x="400" y="150" width="140" height="30" rx="6" fill="#bfe3bf"/><text x="412" y="170" class="t">Domain / Auth</text>
  <rect x="400" y="200" width="140" height="30" rx="6" fill="#bfe3bf"/><text x="403" y="220" class="t">DB / Cache / Net</text>
  <line x1="470" y1="130" x2="470" y2="150" stroke="#3a7a3a" stroke-width="2"/>
  <line x1="470" y1="180" x2="470" y2="200" stroke="#3a7a3a" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="350" y="270" class="t">Dependencies point one direction — down.</text>
  <text x="350" y="286" class="t">Change the DB layer, the UI never notices.</text>
</svg>
:::

The second-system effect is worth internalizing by name because it's a specific, predictable failure of taste, not a vague warning about "over-engineering." It happens exactly when you finally, deeply understand a problem — right after you ship a first version that taught you everything the naive version got wrong — and the temptation is to pour every lesson learned into a second version at once: every abstraction you now see, every edge case you now know about, every piece of flexibility you now wish you'd had. The result is usually more complex than either the naive first version or the disciplined version you'd get by adding lessons one at a time, verified against reality each time. If NeuroQuant's second major refactor starts growing config options and abstraction layers faster than it grows working strategies, that's the pattern showing up in real time, not a coincidence.

::: callout tip
When you disagree with a design and can't immediately articulate why, don't drop the feeling — chase it. Ask "what happens six months from now if this ships as-is?" out loud. Nine times out of ten the vague unease resolves into a specific, arguable claim about coupling, blast radius, or a hidden assumption, and now it's a claim you can defend or discover you were wrong about.
:::

**Build-to-learn:** Find one module in DeskFlow that you'd currently rate as "good taste" and one you'd rate as "needs work." For each, write down the *specific consequence* your feeling is actually predicting — not "this is messy," but "changing the shader pipeline currently requires touching the 3D workspace renderer, the log parser, AND the dashboard, because they all read the same mutable global state." That specificity is the whole exercise.

::: quiz open L4
Describe a piece of code or a design where your gut said "something's off" before you could explain why. What was the concrete consequence your gut was actually predicting, once you chased it down?
rubric: A real, specific example. A named concrete consequence (coupling, blast radius, a leaky abstraction, a hidden shared-state dependency) rather than a restatement of the feeling in different words.
:::

::: quiz mcq L5
A teammate just shipped v1 of a feature and is now proposing a v2 rewrite that adds a plugin system, three new configuration layers, and a generic event bus — all justified by "things we learned building v1." What's the taste-informed response?
- [ ] Approve it — more flexibility is always good engineering
- [ ] Reject it outright — rewrites are always a waste of time
- [x] Ask which of the new abstractions are justified by an actual second concrete use case today, versus ones justified only by "we might need this later" — and cut the rest
- [ ] Approve it as long as it has good test coverage
explain: This is the second-system effect in progress. The taste move isn't blanket approval or blanket rejection — it's separating abstractions earned by a real second use case from ones speculatively built for a future that may never arrive.
:::

