# Directing AI Well
@mastery L4
@prereq debugging-as-a-science technical-writing-communication

:::: grounding
includes: Directing AI systems well — supplying context and constraints, and building an independent verification loop.
know: A model's output is only as verifiable as the fundamentals you hold independently of it. Confident, fluent, and detailed explanations from a language model are not correlated with correctness — a wrong derivation delivered with total confidence is one of the most common failure modes. [ji-hallucination-survey]
know: Supplying tight context and constraints up front — schema, prior decisions, style, what NOT to change — narrows the space of outputs a model explores, the same way a precise type signature narrows the space of valid implementations. [anthropic-prompt-eng]
know: Treat AI output as a draft from a fast, occasionally-confidently-wrong collaborator: verify it against ground truth — running it, testing it, checking the math by hand — before it enters anything load-bearing. [anthropic-prompt-eng]
misconception: Directing AI well means writing a longer, more polite prompt. | Directing AI well means supplying the specific constraints and building the verification loop that make a wrong answer detectable. Length and politeness don't reduce hallucination; structure and checkability do.
misconception: If a model explains its reasoning in confident, technical detail, the reasoning is probably right. | Confidence and technical fluency are not evidence of correctness in model output — they're evidence the model is good at sounding like a technical explanation, which is a different property, and exactly why an independent verification step is mandatory rather than optional.
source: anthropic-prompt-eng | Anthropic, "Prompt Engineering Overview" | https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview
source: ji-hallucination-survey | Ziwei Ji et al., "Survey of Hallucination in Natural Language Generation" | https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.03629
::::

Your North Star document already names this as your core philosophy — "give AI the infrastructure to do the rest" — and this node's only job is to turn that into a named, deliberate, practicable skill instead of an instinct you have but couldn't fully explain to someone else. It's placed last in this part on purpose: it's the one meta-skill built directly on top of two others. Debugging discipline is what makes verifying AI output rigorous instead of a vibe-check ("does this look okay?"). Writing discipline is what makes the context you hand a model precise enough to actually constrain it, instead of vague enough to let it wander.

The whole skill compresses into one loop, run every time AI output is about to become load-bearing — in DeskFlow's codebase, in a NeuroQuant strategy, anywhere the cost of being wrong is more than a wasted five minutes.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A["State the goal + constraints<br>(the fundamentals you already hold)"] --> B["Give the model tight context<br>(files, schema, prior decisions,<br>what NOT to touch)"]
  B --> C["Model drafts a solution"]
  C --> D{"Verify against ground truth:<br>run it, test it, check the math"}
  D -- "fails a check" --> E["Diagnose WHY it failed<br>— using YOUR fundamentals,<br>not the model's explanation"]
  E --> B
  D -- "passes every check" --> F["Ship it —<br>you can defend every line"]
```

Two things in that loop are easy to skip under time pressure, and both are exactly where it breaks. First: the constraints in step A only work if you actually have the fundamentals to state them — "narrow the search space" is a hollow instruction if you don't know what a wrong answer would look like in your domain. This is the whole reason Parts 1–9 come before this node in your curriculum, not after: you cannot direct a model past the edge of what you can independently verify, because past that edge you can't distinguish a right answer from a fluent one. Second: step D has to be *independent* of the model. Asking the same model "are you sure?" is not verification — it's asking someone to grade their own exam, and a model that was confidently wrong the first time has no new information that would make it right the second time you ask. Real verification is external: running the code, executing the test, computing the number by hand, checking the citation actually exists.

::: callout warning
The most dangerous AI-directed mistake isn't a wrong answer you can spot — it's a wrong answer stated with the same fluent confidence as a right one, on a topic just past the edge of what you can check yourself. That's precisely why the verification loop has to be built into your workflow as a default, not reserved for cases where something "feels off" — by the time something feels off, you're already relying on the same fluency-as-confidence signal that just failed you.
:::

This is also where reading discipline (node 10.2) and taste (10.5) quietly feed back in: judging whether a model's proposed *architecture* — not just its code — is sound is a taste call, and recognizing whether it's echoing a genuinely good pattern from training data or a plausible-sounding but wrong one is a reading-discipline call, applied to a different kind of "source" than a paper or a repo.

**Build-to-learn:** Take the next non-trivial task you hand to an AI assistant on DeskFlow or NeuroQuant. Before you send the prompt, write down — separately, for yourself — what a *wrong* answer would look like and how you'd catch it. Then compare that note against what you actually did to verify the output afterward. The gap between the two is exactly the part of this skill you haven't automated yet.

::: quiz mcq L3
An AI assistant gives you a detailed, technically fluent explanation of why a proposed database index will speed up a slow query. What should you do before trusting it?
- [ ] Accept it — the explanation is detailed and internally consistent
- [ ] Ask the same model "are you sure this is right?" and trust whatever it says next
- [x] Run EXPLAIN on the actual query with and without the proposed index and compare
- [ ] Trust it if it cites a real concept like B-trees correctly
explain: Fluency, internal consistency, and correct terminology are not evidence of correctness — and asking the same model to re-check itself isn't independent verification. Only an external, ground-truth check (actually running EXPLAIN) tells you whether the claim holds.
:::

::: quiz open L5
Design a verification step for an AI-proposed change to NeuroQuant's Kelly Criterion position-sizing logic — something where a subtly wrong answer could look completely plausible. What ground truth would you check it against, and why is asking the model to explain its own reasoning insufficient?
rubric: A concrete, external check — e.g., hand-computing the Kelly fraction for a known toy case with a known correct answer, or backtesting the change against a case where the correct sizing behavior is already known. An explanation of why the model re-explaining itself doesn't count as verification: it has no new external signal, so a confidently wrong first answer and a confidently wrong second answer are equally uninformative.
:::

::: layer L5 deeper
**The recursion/parse loop, formalized.** Your own agentic systems work (the "recursion/parse loop" your North Star document already names) is this exact meta-skill, implemented in code instead of practiced by hand: an agent proposes an action, a parser/verifier checks it against ground truth or a schema, and failures route back with a diagnosis instead of a blind retry. The deeper version worth studying is *self-correction reliability* in multi-agent and tool-calling systems — specifically, why naive "ask the model to check its own output" loops tend to converge on confident-but-wrong states (the verifier shares the same blind spots as the generator when they're the same model), and why the fix is almost always an external, non-model check somewhere in the loop: a compiler, a test suite, a schema validator, a second independent model with a narrower job, or a deterministic calculation. This is precisely the "measure, don't ask" principle from your Part 11 curriculum, generalized past vision models to every agentic system you build.
:::

---

# Practicing All Six as One Loop
@mastery L2
@prereq directing-ai-well

:::: grounding
includes: Integrating all six meta-skills into a recurring weekly practice cadence instead of a one-time lesson.
know: A skill practiced once does not survive under pressure. Meta-skills need a recurring slot in a real work cadence to compound — the same way a shipped feature needs a recurring test run to stay shipped, not a one-time check. [ericsson-deliberate-practice]
misconception: Meta-skills are learned once, like a syntax rule, and then permanently "known." | Meta-skills are perishable and contextual. The debugging discipline that feels automatic on a familiar codebase evaporates on a new stack or at 2am under a deadline unless it's practiced as a standing habit, not a lesson you finished once.
source: ericsson-deliberate-practice | K. Anders Ericsson, Ralf Th. Krampe, Clemens Tesch-Römer, "The Role of Deliberate Practice in the Acquisition of Expert Performance" | https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-295X.100.3.363
::::

The six skills in this part don't need a separate practice schedule bolted onto your life — they slot directly into the daily and weekly cadence your North Star document already runs. That's the point of naming them explicitly: once you can see where each one already occurs in your week, you stop treating them as extra work and start treating them as a lens on work you're already doing.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  subgraph Mon["Monday — Plan"]
    S1["10.3 Knowing what NOT<br>to build: pick the week's<br>ONE outcome"]
  end
  subgraph Week["Tue–Fri — Build + Learn"]
    S2["10.1 Debugging as a science:<br>when the build breaks"]
    S3["10.2 Reading source &amp; papers:<br>during the Learn block"]
    S4["10.6 Directing AI well:<br>ambient, every AI-assisted step"]
    S5["10.5 Taste &amp; judgment:<br>ambient, every design choice"]
  end
  subgraph Sat["Saturday — Ship &amp; Show"]
    S6["10.4 Technical writing:<br>the release note /<br>build-in-public post"]
  end
  Mon --> Week --> Sat
```

The honest test of whether any of this landed isn't whether you can recite the six names back — it's whether, three months from now, your own AI-coaching loop (the README at the top of your North Star doc) scores you higher on these than it does today, based on what you actually *demonstrate*, not what you claim to know. That scoring mechanism already exists in your workflow; this lesson just gave it something real to measure.

::: callout tip
When you next run your AI Coaching README loop on Part 10, don't just talk about the concepts — walk through a real example for each skill from the last two weeks of actual DeskFlow or NeuroQuant work. "I can name the six meta-skills" is L1. "I can point to a specific Tuesday where I used the bisection method on a real bug" is L3 or higher. The gap between those two answers is the whole gap this part exists to close.
:::
